<article class="env-lemma" id="04E8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04E8">Lemma <span data-tag="04E8">10.148.4</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R \to S$ is formally unramified, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R \to S_{\mathfrak q}$ is formally unramified for all primes $\mathfrak q$ of $S$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R_{\mathfrak p} \to S_{\mathfrak q}$ is formally unramified for all primes $\mathfrak q$ of $S$ with $\mathfrak p = R \cap \mathfrak q$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have seen in Lemma <a href="/tag/00UO" data-tag="00UO">10.148.3</a> that (1) is equivalent to $\Omega _{S/R} = 0$. Similarly, by Lemma <a href="/tag/00RT" data-tag="00RT">10.131.8</a> we see that (2) and (3) are equivalent to $(\Omega _{S/R})_{\mathfrak q} = 0$ for all $\mathfrak q$. Hence the equivalence follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/00HN" data-tag="00HN">10.23.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>